        <p>much worse has to follow. The correspondent re- ferred to, has, attr his white to mine of the stllitied districts end committal°. with eniiiient Indian officials, come to Cie eenelizeion that thin will be the meted" Monne of this centnry. It will, he believes, greatly eto peas that of 1876 both area itnil in severity. Now, what dem this mem WO lures On a previous °Math a men- tioned that no fewer than five million pe sena died of atarvatiou during the Moline of twenty years ago, lf, themfore, the bonnie of this year stsrpesses that of 1876, it will, other dome beiog equal, came the death of mote than five million. of our Indian fellow subjects. But the question may well be tie'ved, are other things to Fe *goal F Is there to be no satiation, no im- provement, in the methods of relief to be adopted It must be admitted that, up to • eertom pond at all events, there hue linen an ominous similarity in the official way of dealing with the two hafnium Both in this country and in :who thorn has keen jost as there wee in 18711, a tendency on the port of those who ! are most directly responsible to minimise ths. serionstiese of the situation in India. It is only • few weeks since that Lord Groom thnitar,r, the Kecrotauy for India in the present Govern- ment, discouraged the opening of relief funds in this country. That extraordinary action on the port of the rempomible Minister of the Crown willt in view of later reports, need • It of explaining.l GINVII to show that Lord Canton 11•MILioN man either lanientabiy ignorant of the state of things in the ;frost depetplency which is under his charge, or that, Micawber-like, he weakly and M. eptly waited fur something to turn up to avert a groat calamity. There is now plenty of evidence to show that in India• famine was anticipated and piovided against in certain directions malty months ago. Some information bearing upon the point, supplied by • sergeant-major of carat.) who returned from India recently, was published in the i Liverpool Doily Poet" on Wednesday. According to this offioer it Was known eighteen months ago that them would he • famine, and acting opon thin knowledgii the Cointniesanat Departmoot of the Army lifitmht up corn in all direction. Ex. Retold prices sere paid in order to lay op a stiffici•iit foul supply for the soldiers during the time of scarcity,eial as a notarial resolt many district +MAI are lerw lit tior torus. of famine Were denuded of their fimil-stotri, WIIOII the officer was trovelling through loth&amp; on hie way to Eugionti the railway stations were crowded with starving poople, and naturally when ha arrived in this muoitry he was surprool that there shiaMl be any acepticisio as to the extent of the difficulty. That oeptimsin was entirely doe to the extraord- inary otriptid optimism which has been already onn. montiid 'opal. Tim repot II or of oubecriptione whizli is t.king place ui all p at, of the country show that the English people am Son thoroogl.ly aline to the necemitim of the es.", Bit, it Or to</p>
