        <head>E PRESIDENTIAL ELECTION AND AFTER.</head>
        <p>E PRESIDENTIAL ELECTION AND AFTER.</p>
        <p>Br Dons N. Jot.,</p>
        <p>XVI.—JERICHO. SS. ,Helnieht It is rather a new thiog to take • earner. party d.,•rn the road lune Jerusalem to Jericho, and 0 had been de- signed that we ehould all go on horseback. but Mr. Lunn announnel n Tueedav night that owing to the Sultan'. having requiwtionrall a certain number of homes frr Bervlce in the N.orth .d Fales•lue, .t eras impossible I to get enough for our party,and that part of Um I would have to drier. ii none fus eeeretly rej seed at I the turn thlnga hod taken, to, to au inesperienced eivelier, .it limns In the saddle under a blazing Syrian</p>
        <p>We noon found ourselves In the tiering of ravine+ through which the rod winds down to the plain of Jericho. The total demerit le 3.500 feet, and for aboul half the way the Russian tower on Mount Olivet can be aeen In the dietance. The road ...wee se t rernendnue zigzags among the hills which rite on either side of the road, br,ern after tlac long drought and wrinkled with atone., with the white limentone rock eh .rlog through in many Vac. Oar chief •inugemont during the des- ' cent gran watching the consummate horsemanship of Ige. flcword, of Jiffs, who went with us part of thy gray, mounted on his auperb grey Arab, and the Moth who headed our ...wort. To sew them raring their bargee on the eteep slope of the hill. WWI the aluilt's white kellyth Bring out behind him, reininded one of the Calisadong tale.. In the midst of • wild glen we came upon She fountain of Es..thamegh, whirl, hi one of the boundary mark. between Judah and Benjamin, and affords the only drinkable water for the whole way down to Jrri,ho.</p>
